    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Iraq resumes shipping oil for export to Turkey via pipeline",
    summary: "Iraq resumes shipping oil for export to Turkey via pipeline Submitted by MEE staff on Tue, 11/17/2026 - 20:00 Iraqi oil minister says Kirkuk-Ceyhan pipeline could reach capacity of 500,000 bpd with repairs and upgrades An Iraqi government forces member walks next to a pipeline at the Bai Hassan Oil Field, west of the northern Iraqi city of Kirkuk, on 19 October 2017 (Ahmad al-Rubaye/AFP) Off Iraq has resumed the pumping of crude oil for export through the Kirkuk-Ceyhan Pipeline to Turkey , as it looks to bypass the Strait of Hormuz. Iraq plans to pump up to 120,000 barrels per day (bpd) of oil through the 960km-long pipeline that runs from the Iraqi city to Turkey’s Mediterranean port, Iraqi Oil Minister Bassem Khudair said in a statement reported by the state-owned Iraqi News Agency on Tuesday. He said the flow could reach 500,000 bpd after “successful completion of maintenance, rehabilitation, technical and operational tests” on the pipeline, which has been shuttered for more than a decade. Iraq is one of the countries hardest hit by the US and Iran’s competing blockades in the Strait of Hormuz. Nearly all of Iraq’s oil exports exited the waterway before the US-Israeli war on Iran, and revenue from sales contributed to 90 percent of Iraq’s budget. Restarting oil flows from Kirkuk to Ceyhan is the first step in Iraq’s efforts to reduce its dependence on Hormuz. Baghdad wants to connect Basra, its energy hub in southern Iraq, to the Mediterranean via the Syrian port of Baniyas and Turkey’s port of Ceyhan. Middle East Eye was the first to report US efforts to revive a pipeline connection between Kirkuk and Baniyas. US ambassador to Iraq Tom Barrack hailed the reopening of the Kirkuk-Ceyhan line on Tuesday in a post on social media. “Go Iraq, Go,” Barrack wrote . US Centcom commander expected in Saudi Arabia with dozens of air defence experts, sources say om the semiautonomous Kurdistan region after a two-year dispute. That agreement saw the Kurdistan regional government (KRG) and foreign oil producers operating in the region agreeing to allow 180,000 to 190,000 bpd of crude to flow to Turkiye’s Ceyhan port. But Tuesday’s announcement will allow for oil from Iraq’s federal government to flow directly to Ceyhan. Turkey has been pushing for a direct connection between Basra and Ceyhan. The Kirkuk pipeline has a capacity of more than 1.5 million bpd. Energy industry experts tell MEE that tens of billions of dollars will be spent on new infrastructure in the coming years to get around Hormuz. The UAE is leading the way in building pipelines around the chokepoint. But recent attacks on Saudi Arabia's East-West Pipeline attributed to the Houthis and militias in Iraq underscore how those workarounds remain vulnerable. Energy News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
    preview: "Iraq resumes shipping oil for export to Turkey via pipeline Submitted by MEE staff on Tue, 11/17/2026 - 20:00 Iraqi oil minister says Kirkuk-Ceyhan pipeline could reach capacity of 500,000 bpd with repairs and upgrades An Iraqi government forces member walks next to a pipeline at the Bai Hassan Oil Field, west of the northern Iraqi city of Kirkuk, on 19 October 2017 (Ahmad al-Rubaye/AFP) Off Iraq has resumed the pumping of crude oil for export through the Kirkuk-Ceyhan Pipeline to Turkey , as it looks to bypass the Strait of Hormuz.",
    body: "Iraq resumes shipping oil for export to Turkey via pipeline Submitted by MEE staff on Tue, 11/17/2026 - 20:00 Iraqi oil minister says Kirkuk-Ceyhan pipeline could reach capacity of 500,000 bpd with repairs and upgrades An Iraqi government forces member walks next to a pipeline at the Bai Hassan Oil Field, west of the northern Iraqi city of Kirkuk, on 19 October 2017 (Ahmad al-Rubaye/AFP) Off Iraq has resumed the pumping of crude oil for export through the Kirkuk-Ceyhan Pipeline to Turkey , as it looks to bypass the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nIraq plans to pump up to 120,000 barrels per day (bpd) of oil through the 960km-long pipeline that runs from the Iraqi city to Turkey’s Mediterranean port, Iraqi Oil Minister Bassem Khudair said in a statement reported by the state-owned Iraqi News Agency on Tuesday. He said the flow could reach 500,000 bpd after “successful completion of maintenance, rehabilitation, technical and operational tests” on the pipeline, which has been shuttered for more than a decade.\n\nIraq is one of the countries hardest hit by the US and Iran’s competing blockades in the Strait of Hormuz. Nearly all of Iraq’s oil exports exited the waterway before the US-Israeli war on Iran, and revenue from sales contributed to 90 percent of Iraq’s budget. Restarting oil flows from Kirkuk to Ceyhan is the first step in Iraq’s efforts to reduce its dependence on Hormuz. Baghdad wants to connect Basra, its energy hub in southern Iraq, to the Mediterranean via the Syrian port of Baniyas and Turkey’s port of Ceyhan.\n\nMiddle East Eye was the first to report US efforts to revive a pipeline connection between Kirkuk and Baniyas. US ambassador to Iraq Tom Barrack hailed the reopening of the Kirkuk-Ceyhan line on Tuesday in a post on social media. “Go Iraq, Go,” Barrack wrote . US Centcom commander expected in Saudi Arabia with dozens of air defence experts, sources say om the semiautonomous Kurdistan region after a two-year dispute.\n\nThat agreement saw the Kurdistan regional government (KRG) and foreign oil producers operating in the region agreeing to allow 180,000 to 190,000 bpd of crude to flow to Turkiye’s Ceyhan port. But Tuesday’s announcement will allow for oil from Iraq’s federal government to flow directly to Ceyhan. Turkey has been pushing for a direct connection between Basra and Ceyhan. The Kirkuk pipeline has a capacity of more than 1.5 million bpd.\n\nEnergy industry experts tell MEE that tens of billions of dollars will be spent on new infrastructure in the coming years to get around Hormuz. The UAE is leading the way in building pipelines around the chokepoint. But recent attacks on Saudi Arabia's East-West Pipeline attributed to the Houthis and militias in Iraq underscore how those workarounds remain vulnerable. Energy News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
